Subject: [PATCH] calibrated-output bounds: no calibrator may publish certainty
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Field failure on the owner's Mac (LOG 32): the patch-0019 walk fixture
published p_model == 1.0 exactly — a saturated PLATT step (slope -59 on
3 separable validation rows) meeting 4-decimal rounding; the sandbox had
passed the same test by numeric luck. Same pathology family as the
July-2025 isotonic episode (LOG 31), different calibrator, which settles
the hardening choice: an isotonic-vs-Platt margin rule fixes neither.

Two independent bounds, both pinned by tests that construct the
saturating fits explicitly: calibrator transforms clip to
[CAL_OUTPUT_CLIP, 1-CAL_OUTPUT_CLIP] = [0.005, 0.995] (the measured
healthy-Platt range, r41); published probabilities round then clamp one
rounding-ulp inside (0,1), because series aggregation defeats the map
clip alone (Bo5 of 0.995s = 0.999999 -> rounds to 1.0000). Plus
BUNDLE_BEHAVIOR_REV in the version hash: the run-once gate correctly
refused a --replace after a pure code change produced an identical
version — behavior changes are now detectable.

Measured: static report moves three fourth-decimal digits; the hardened
sandbox walk improves exactly where the diagnosis said (tier2 2025H2
0.7231 -> 0.6998; July monthly 0.8096 -> 0.7608; published range
[0.0001, 1.0000] -> [0.0003, 0.9999]; 2026H2 cells byte-identical), and
Elo still leads 3 of 4 tiers over the full walk — the finding stands,
the certainty claims are gone. Decided BEFORE the first official
backtest run (no published result existed to tune against);
pre-hardening validation result archived by the gate. ASSUMPTIONS §17;
tests 101 -> 106.
